      <p>Sri Lanka is one of the world's most rewarding destinations for a first-time visitor — compact enough to cover a lot of ground in two weeks, varied enough to feel like three countries in one, and genuinely, deeply welcoming in a way that is not simply a product of the tourist industry. Ancient ruins, wildlife safaris, world-class surf beaches, misty hill country tea estates and some of the most extraordinary food in Asia are all within a few hours of each other.</p>

      <p>This guide covers everything a first-time visitor needs to know before they arrive: visa, best time to go, how to get around, budget, safety and the classic two-week circuit that most first-timers use as a framework.</p>

      <h2>Is Sri Lanka Right for You?</h2>

      <p>Sri Lanka rewards travellers who are comfortable with heat, some degree of improvisation and food that is genuinely spicy. It is not a destination where everything is pristinely organised — tuk-tuks break down, buses run late, the electricity occasionally fails, and haggling is expected in tourist markets. In return, the country offers scenery and experiences of extraordinary quality at prices that are very low by global standards.</p>

      <p>If you want a relaxed beach holiday with all-inclusive hotels, the south and west coasts (particularly Bentota, Beruwala and the Negombo strip) can accommodate that. If you want an active cultural itinerary that takes in ancient cities, wildlife, mountains and local food, Sri Lanka will not disappoint. Most first-timers try to do both — and two weeks is enough time to do it properly.</p>

      <h2>Visa</h2>

      <p>Most nationalities require an Electronic Travel Authorization (ETA) to enter Sri Lanka. The process is straightforward: apply online at eta.gov.lk at least 48 hours before travel, pay the fee (currently USD 20 for a single-entry tourist visa, USD 35 for double-entry, waived for some nationalities — check the website), and receive your ETA by email. Print it or save it to your phone. The visa allows a 30-day stay, extendable at the Department of Immigration in Colombo.</p>

      <p>Check the official eta.gov.lk site before travelling — policies change periodically. Be wary of third-party websites that charge premium fees for the same ETA; use only the official government portal.</p>

      <h2>When to Go</h2>

      <p>Sri Lanka has two monsoon seasons that affect different parts of the island at different times, which means that in theory some part of the island is always in good weather. In practice, most first-time visitors aim for the December–April window, when the south and west coasts (the most popular beach areas), the Cultural Triangle and the hill country are all in dry season.</p>

      <p>May–September is the southwest monsoon — the south and west coasts get significant rain, but the east coast (Trincomalee, Arugam Bay) is in its dry season and excellent for surfing and beach holidays. October–November is the inter-monsoon, with occasional showers across much of the island.</p>

      <p>The "shoulder" months of November and May can be excellent value — fewer crowds, lower prices, and weather that is less predictable but often perfectly fine. See our <a href="/blog/best-time-visit-sri-lanka">detailed guide to the best time to visit</a> for a month-by-month breakdown.</p>

      <h2>How Long to Spend</h2>

      <p><strong>1 week:</strong> Possible but rushed. Focus on one region — either the Cultural Triangle + hill country, or the south coast. You won't be able to do both justice.</p>

      <p><strong>2 weeks:</strong> The sweet spot for a first visit. Enough time to cover the classic circuit — Cultural Triangle, hill country train journey, south coast beach — with time to slow down and actually enjoy each place rather than rushing through.</p>

      <p><strong>3+ weeks:</strong> Add the east coast (Trincomalee, Arugam Bay), Jaffna in the north, or slower travel through the hill country. Sri Lanka rewards slower travel significantly.</p>

      <h2>The Classic Two-Week Circuit</h2>

      <p>This is the route that most first-time visitors follow, and it works well for a reason — it hits the highlights in a logical geographical order without excessive backtracking:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Days 1–2: Colombo</strong> — arrive, recover from the flight, eat well, explore the city</li>
        <li><strong>Days 3–4: Negombo or drive north</strong> — beach day, then up to the Cultural Triangle</li>
        <li><strong>Days 5–7: Cultural Triangle</strong> — Sigiriya, Dambulla, Anuradhapura or Polonnaruwa; base in Habarana or Sigiriya village</li>
        <li><strong>Days 8–9: Kandy</strong> — temple of the tooth, Peradeniya botanical gardens, Knuckles foothills</li>
        <li><strong>Days 10–11: Hill Country</strong> — train from Kandy to Ella via Nuwara Eliya, Nine Arch Bridge, Ella Rock</li>
        <li><strong>Days 12–13: South Coast</strong> — Mirissa, Tangalle or Weligama for beaches and whale watching</li>
        <li><strong>Day 14: Galle</strong> — Galle Fort, then back to Colombo for departure</li>
      </ol>

      <p>This itinerary involves significant travel distances — Sri Lanka is compact but road speeds are slow. Budget 3–5 hours for most inter-city transfers. Our <a href="/blog/2-weeks-in-sri-lanka-itinerary">full two-week itinerary</a> covers this route in detail with specific accommodation recommendations and day-by-day planning.</p>

      <h2>Getting Around</h2>

      <p><strong>Private driver/car hire:</strong> The most comfortable and efficient option for the Cultural Triangle and south coast. A private air-conditioned car with driver costs USD 60–90 per day — expensive by local standards but shared between two to four people it is excellent value. Your driver becomes an invaluable guide to local restaurants, hidden viewpoints and practical shortcuts.</p>

      <p><strong>Train:</strong> Sri Lanka's trains are slow, often late, and genuinely excellent for scenic journeys. The Kandy–Ella train is one of the great rail journeys of Asia. Book observation car seats months in advance during peak season. The Colombo–Galle coastal line is also worth taking.</p>

      <p><strong>Bus:</strong> The cheapest option, covering virtually every town in the country. Express buses are faster and air-conditioned; regular buses are slower and hot but genuinely local. Good for independent travellers on a tight budget but not recommended as the primary transport mode for a two-week first visit.</p>

      <p><strong>Tuk-tuk:</strong> Essential for getting around within towns and for short-distance excursions. Always agree a price before getting in, or use a metered tuk-tuk in Colombo via apps like PickMe or Uber (both work well in the capital).</p>

      <h2>Budget</h2>

      <p>Sri Lanka is not as cheap as it was before the 2022 economic crisis, which caused significant price inflation across the board. Nevertheless, it remains very affordable by Western European or North American standards.</p>

