      <p>The biggest variable cost is accommodation — beach-town prices are significantly higher than inland prices. Wildlife safaris (USD 30–80 per person) and temple entry fees (USD 25–30 per site at major archaeological sites) add up quickly in the Cultural Triangle and are worth building into your budget.</p>

      <p>See our full <a href="/blog/sri-lanka-budget-travel">Sri Lanka budget travel guide</a> for a detailed breakdown by category.</p>

      <h2>Money</h2>

      <p>The Sri Lankan rupee (LKR) is the currency. At the time of writing, USD 1 = approximately LKR 300–320 (check current rates before travelling). Cash is king outside Colombo and the main tourist towns — carry LKR for small guesthouses, local restaurants, tuk-tuks and market shopping.</p>

      <p>ATMs are widely available in Colombo, Kandy, Galle and tourist areas. In smaller towns and the Cultural Triangle, they are less reliable — draw cash before leaving a major town. USD, EUR and GBP can be exchanged at banks and licensed money changers; avoid the unlicensed changers who approach you at tourist sites.</p>

      <h2>Safety</h2>

      <p>Sri Lanka is generally a safe country for tourists. Violent crime against visitors is rare. The main practical risks are:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Traffic:</strong> Sri Lankan roads are dangerous — the combination of narrow roads, fast buses and tuk-tuks weaving through traffic makes road accidents the most significant safety risk. Avoid night driving where possible.</li>
        <li><strong>Touts and scams:</strong> Common at tourist sites, particularly Colombo and Sigiriya. Gem scams (being taken to a gem shop and pressured to buy at inflated prices), "free" cultural shows that turn out to be heavily commercialised, and drivers who take you to their cousin's guesthouse rather than your booked accommodation are the most common. A polite but firm "no thank you" handles most situations.</li>
        <li><strong>Sun and heat:</strong> The combination of tropical heat and reflective white limestone at ancient sites causes sunstroke and severe sunburn faster than visitors expect. Wide-brimmed hats, sunscreen and staying hydrated are non-optional.</li>
        <li><strong>Sea conditions:</strong> Several beaches (particularly on the south and west coasts during the monsoon) have dangerous rip currents. Swim only at beaches with lifeguards and within the flagged areas. Check local advice before entering the sea.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Health and Vaccinations</h2>

      <p>Consult a travel health clinic at least 4–6 weeks before travelling. Commonly recommended vaccinations for Sri Lanka include hepatitis A, typhoid, tetanus and rabies (particularly if you plan to be in rural areas). Malaria prophylaxis may be recommended for some itineraries — the main Cultural Triangle and south coast circuit carries low malaria risk, but remote areas near the north and east carry moderate risk. Check current NHS or CDC guidance.</p>

      <p>Drink only sealed bottled water. Avoid ice in local restaurants (most tourist-area restaurants use purified ice, but the simplest rule is to avoid it unless you're sure). Food safety at busy local restaurants is generally good — the food is freshly cooked — but street food from slow or uncovered stalls carries higher risk.</p>

      <h2>Cultural Etiquette</h2>

      <p><strong>Dress at temples:</strong> Remove shoes at all Buddhist and Hindu temples. Cover shoulders and knees before entering — a light scarf or sarong is worth carrying. At Anuradhapura, Polonnaruwa and other major sites, the rules are strictly enforced and sarong rental is available at the entrance.</p>

      <p><strong>Posing with Buddha statues:</strong> Photographs with your back to a Buddha statue (facing the camera) are considered disrespectful and may result in arrest — this is enforced by law. Pose beside a statue, not in front of it with your back to it.</p>

      <p><strong>Right hand:</strong> Use your right hand for eating, passing objects and greeting. The left hand is traditionally considered unclean.</p>

      <p><strong>Head wobble:</strong> The side-to-side head wobble means yes/agreement/acknowledgement. It takes some getting used to but you will start doing it yourself within a week.</p>

      <p><strong>Bargaining:</strong> Expected and accepted in markets and with tuk-tuk drivers who quote without a meter. Not appropriate in restaurants, hotels or shops with fixed prices displayed. A gentle negotiation rather than aggressive bargaining is the correct approach.</p>

